{ Hello everyone, and welcome to my study on the Semi-Slav. The Semi-Slav is one of the best opening against d4. It is played by many many grandmasters, such as Viswanathan Anand. Today I will be giving you the ideas in the variations of the Semi-Slav. Let's start. }
1. d4 { White opens the game with d4, a great move. Let's not go crazy for now and play classically. Respond with d5! } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd2d4,Bd7d5] } 1... d5 { This looks boring, but things will be exciting later. White plays the Queen's Gambit. } { [%csl Gd5,Rd4][%cal Gd7d5] } 2. c4 { Enter the Slav Defense with c6! } { [%csl Yc4,Yd4,Bd5][%cal Rc2c4,Bc7c6,Bc6d5] } 2... c6 { Nf3 is the best response for white against the Slav. } { [%csl Gd5,Gc6,Rd4,Rc4][%cal Gc7c6] } 3. Nf3 { Let's develop our g8-knight. } { [%csl Rf3,Rd4,Bg8][%cal Rg1f3] } 3... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 4. Nc3 { White develops his b1 knight to c3. Let's enter the Semi-Slav with e6! } { [%csl Rc3,Rf3,Yd4,Yc4,Gf6,Gd5,Gc6,Gb7][%cal Rb1c3,Be7e6] } 4... e6 { This is the start of the Semi-Slav. This might look like a London System with a bad c8 bishop, but we will find a way to develop it. Our position is asleep right now, but after a few moves our position will wake up and spring to life, and white's pieces will be forced backwards. Let's see how. } { [%csl Gf7,Ge6,Gd5,Gc6,Gb7,Bf6,Yf3,Yc3,Rc4,Rd4,Rc8] } *

