{ Get to the Semi-Slav position. }
1. d4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd2d4] } 1... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 2. c4 { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rc2c4] } 2... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 3. Nf3 { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rg1f3] } 3... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 4. Nc3 { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rb1c3] } 4... e6 { This is the Semi-Slav. White can play 5. Bg5 to attack our knight. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6,Bc1g5] } 5. Bg5 { Develop the b8 knight to the center. } { [%csl Bb8][%cal Gg5d8] } 5... Nbd7 { [%csl Gd7,Gf6][%cal Gb8d7] } 6. e3 { Now we can play Qa5, activating the queen. } { [%csl Re3][%cal Re2e3,Bd8a5,Yf1c4] } 6... Qa5 { The queen is active. Notice how black is threatening Ne4, attacking the g5 bishop and putting pressure on c3. } { [%csl Ga5,Yc3,Yg5][%cal Gd8a5,Ba5e1,Ba5h5,Yf6e4,Ye4g5,Ye4c3] } 7. cxd5 { Recapture with knight } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc4d5,Bf6d5] } 7... Nxd5 { If our opponent plays Bd3 before taking on d5, we can take on c4 first and develop the bishop on c8 like I showed you earlier on. With a great position, we can now play b6 and Ba6 to trade of our bad c8 bishop with a very nice position. The Semi-Slav always leads to a nice position! } { [%csl Gd5,Gf1][%cal Gf6d5,Bb7b6,Bc8a6,Ga6f1] } *

